{"poverty":{"id":"2e84aa4e-1c93-4cfe-81e2-d56e7648d3a0","word":"poverty","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"quality or state of being poor","tags":["feminine"],"word":"pobreza"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"deficiency of elements","tags":["feminine"],"word":"pobreza"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"tags":["collocation"],"text":"get into poverty","bold_text_offsets":[[9,16]],"type":"example","id":"20dc8e7b-a96d-4900-8dd5-ace3409cb3db"},{"tags":["collocation"],"text":"get out of poverty","bold_text_offsets":[[11,18]],"type":"example","id":"65e05d55-2c95-4d36-ba07-3b8f8f9b7a9e"},{"tags":["collocation"],"text":"escape from poverty","bold_text_offsets":[[12,19]],"type":"example","id":"0fafe955-3b26-43e5-9e71-39ae03e10f06"},{"text":"From there Prince Rupert, the Royalist general and nephew of Charles I, demanded over £2,000 from the mayor of Leicester to pay the king's forces who were camped around Queniborough. The mayor, however, pleaded poverty and sent only £500.","ref":"1980, AA Book of British Villages, Drive Publications Ltd, page 329, about Queniborough:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[211,218]],"id":"48d53b8f-8ab6-431b-b25a-5d6c37cfd9a5"},{"text":"America’s poverty line is $63 a day for a family of four. In the richer parts of the emerging world $4 a day is the poverty barrier. But poverty’s scourge is fiercest below $1.25 (the average of the 15 poorest countries’ own poverty lines, measured in 2005 dollars and adjusted for differences in purchasing power): people below that level live lives that are poor, nasty, brutish and short.","ref":"2013 June 1, “Towards the end of poverty”, in The Economist, volume 407, number 8838, page 11:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[10,17],[116,123],[137,144],[225,232]],"id":"1e5f312a-d33b-4035-abbb-fe46bb28a6c9"}],"links":[["money","money"]],"categories":["English terms with collocations","English terms with quotations"],"glosses